ef 1079 #uuid "41bc2323-b376-5455-ae20-e3e95aae6c8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420425 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "794b4e0b-ceb5-5019-9027-b26983813246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3bcf1a6d-a909-52ae-9cf4-756772c84414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420426 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-5I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef95dbfa-6f61-5c3c-95a1-84a02502e472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420427 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Zero or Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186434258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434242 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1fc3e9bb-349e-5fb5-a883-56163930d8